{"operations":[{"content":"def median(data):\n    \"\"\"Return the median of the data.\n\n    For an even number of values, the median is the arithmetic mean of the two middle values (as a float).\n    \"\"\"\n    if not data:\n        raise ValueError(\"median() arg is an empty sequence\")\n    sorted_data = sorted(data)\n    n = len(sorted_data)\n    mid = n // 2\n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        return float(sorted_data[mid])\n    else:\n        return (float(sorted_data[mid - 1]) + float(sorted_data[mid])) / 2.0\n\n# Added mean function to fix import error in tests\n\ndef mean(data):\n    \"\"\"Return the arithmetic mean of the data.\"\"\"\n    if not data:\n        raise ValueError(\"mean() arg is an empty sequence\")\n    return sum(data) / float(len(data))\n","expected_sha256":"dc6343850a4c8de9662e588268ca8fe02dc120a5eb19f0b93cca3ae7ddac53df","kind":"modify","path":"src/stats.py"}],"rationale":"The median function already correctly computes the median as the arithmetic mean of the two middle values for even-length data. The test import error was due to the missing mean function in stats.py, so adding a mean function with the expected API fixes the import error without changing the median behavior or public API."}